WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:01.180
My name is Angus McFadyen

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.200
I'm a silversmith an engraver.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.200
I've been doing it since 1984.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.760
Engraving is one of these things that when it becomes know that you engrave,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.160
then you get asked to do jobs like this, make a sundial replica.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.720
Engraving is basically drawing on metal.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.720
So the first thing you have to do is to draw on the metal and the simplest thing to use is a pencil.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.080
Then once you've drawn on the metal, you need to mark it in more permanently before you start engraving.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.880
So we use a scribe to do that

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:54.080
that you can then follow with the graver.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.840
And the graver is cutting metal so you're removing metal

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.080
which leaves you with a line of sorts of depths or widths according to the job.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.440
Very versatile process.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:22.000
Brass is a material that is used a lot particularly for this type of thing, instruments in the past.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.440
This brass for this sundial actually, although it is machining brass which has quite a high lead content

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.600
in the alloy, it's actually quite hard to cut.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.160
Engraving is it extremely time-consuming.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.080
There are no shortcuts, there's nowhere to
hide, there's no cheating.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.320
You've got to do it as nicely as you possibly can.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.520
It can be tiring when you're on a big job and it's just day in day out, cutting away,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.080
but at the same time, I love it.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.520
And I think if you didn't enjoy doing it it
would just be a chore

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.000
and you wouldn't do totally good work I don't think either.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.200
Even now when you do a cut that you're really pleased with, it's quite exciting still.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.360
It was really fascinating to look closely at the original sundial.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.440
There are marks at the beginnings or ends of some of the cuts

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.040
which most people probably wouldn't even notice

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.640
and they're not mistakes, they're not errors but they're marks which probably only an engraver will notice.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.840
There's this instant connect with the person who did it and that's lovely.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:43.360
You know, to go back that many years and just have instant connection with the person who did it.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.320
And you say 'oh yeah, I know what was going on there, I know what you did there.'

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:52.320
That personal touch is something you only get when someone's sat down and done it themselves.

